          title: "Mary’s extravagance",
          body: "The gesture is breathtaking in its abandon. A **Roman pound** (about 327 grams) of **pure nard** — an aromatic oil imported from the Himalayas — worth **three hundred denarii**, nearly a year’s wages for a laborer (v. 5). Mary pours the whole flask over Jesus’ feet and, in an act of stunning humility, wipes them with her *hair* — a woman’s hair, unbound in public, her glory used as a towel. This is not calculation but worship: love that does not count the cost because the beloved is beyond price. John links her to the sister who *“sat at his feet”* to listen (Luke 10:39) and to the grieving woman who *“fell at his feet”* (11:32); at his feet is simply where Mary belongs. The whole **house is filled with the fragrance** — a detail John lingers over, as if the aroma of one soul’s reckless devotion still hangs in the air of the church. Set against Judas’ cold arithmetic in the next verse, her extravagance is the truer wisdom.",
