      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a12.13.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g12.13.5', 'g12.13.13', 'g12.13.22'],
          lemma: "τὰ βαΐα … Ὡσαννά … ὁ Βασιλεύς",
          title: "Hosanna to the King",
          body: "The crowd greets Jesus with the liturgy of Psalm 118, the pilgrim psalm sung at the great feasts: **“Hosanna! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.”** *Hosanna* began as a prayer — *“Save now!”* — but had become a shout of praise; the crowd waves **palm branches**, a nationalist emblem of victory since the Maccabees, and adds a line the psalm does not have — **“the King of Israel!”** They are right and they are wrong. He *is* the King; but they crown him for the wrong throne, dreaming of a liberator who will drive out Rome, when he rides toward a cross. He answers their misplaced hope not by refusing the title but by *reframing* it: he finds a young donkey and sits on it (v. 14), enacting Zechariah’s prophecy of a king who comes *humble,* bringing peace, not the warhorse they wanted. Within the week these same crowds, their political hopes disappointed, may well be among those crying *“Crucify.”* The palm-waving is real, but a King who saves by dying is not the King they came out to hail.",
        },
      ],
